# What the registers don't measure — and why your analysis must withstand scrutiny
prompt: "Why do more registered diagnoses and fee codes not necessarily mean more disease? What is small-cell suppression, and why must cells with few patients be suppressed before you publish the figure? Who decides who may link these registers — and what really happened to the Health Analytics Platform? Why is your notebook part of the method, not an appendix to it?"
enroll: https://drawcast.anvil.app

chapter: "What the registers actually measure"

## The rising curve
lang: en
voice: female
level: basic
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 2016
      - 2017
      - 2018
      - 2019
      - 2020
      - 2021
      - 2022
      - 2023
    series:
      - {name: E11 contacts (npr), values: [100, 106, 113, 121, 128, 139, 148, 158]}
      - {name: GP fee codes (kuhr), values: [100, 105, 111, 118, 124, 133, 141, 149]}
      - {name: Patients with E11, values: [100, 102, 104, 106, 108, 109, 111, 112]}
      - {name: New metformin users (lmr), values: [100, 101, 99, 102, 100, 103, 101, 102]}
    ylim:
      - 90
      - 170
    x_label: Year
    y_label: Index (2016 = 100)
    title: 'Eight years in the cohort: everything rises'

    text naiv "“Norway got sicker”" #b5482e x 300 y 610 hidden true
    mark naiv_strike target naiv kind strike hidden true
    text act3_title "What lies behind one npr row?" x 500 y 700 font_size 30 hidden true
    box target "More register rows" x 750 y 440 hidden true
    box c1 "Better coding" x 230 y 640 hidden true
    box c2 "Money follows codes" x 230 y 510 hidden true
    box c3 "New reporting rules" x 230 y 380 hidden true
    box c4 "More disease?" #b5482e x 230 y 240 hidden true
    arrow a1 c1 -> target hidden true
    arrow a2 c2 -> target hidden true
    arrow a3 c3 -> target hidden true
    arrow a4 c4 -> target #b5482e hidden true

This lecture is about a mistake you can publish without noticing: reading a register curve as if it measured disease.
    draw title axes

In our cohort of adults with type 2 diabetes, the number of E11 contacts in npr rises from index 100 in 2016 to 158 in 2023.
    draw line_1

    pause 0.3

Fifty-eight percent more contacts in eight years — and the sentence almost writes itself.
    draw naiv

kuhr points the same way: GP fee codes for diabetes check-ups rise to 149.
    draw line_2

Two registers, collected by entirely different players, with almost the same rise — that is exactly what makes the trap convincing.
    highlight line_1 line_2 effect glow

    pause 0.5

Then we count patients instead of contacts: people with at least one E11 code rise only to 112.
    draw line_3

And new metformin users in lmr — those with no A10 in the washout window — stay flat around 100.
    draw line_4

Incidence is standing still. No more new diabetics are entering the cohort.
    focus line_4

Fifty-eight percent more rows, twelve percent more patients, zero more new users — the same people, recorded more often.
    point at.ref line_1 gesture tap

The rising curve is a curve of registrations.
    draw naiv_strike delivery grave

    pause 0.5

    clear

A row in npr is not a disease — it is a registration, and registrations have many parents.
    draw act3_title target

Coding practice: the doctor who used to write “check-up” now writes E11.9. Same patient, new row.
    draw c1 a1

Money: activity-based financing, ISF, introduced in 1997, pays hospitals by the diagnoses and procedures they register.
    draw c2 a2

And reporting changes — new requirements and new fee codes create breaks in the time series that look like changes in health.
    draw c3 a3

Real disease may of course lie behind it too. But all four arrows end in the same column.
    draw c4 a4

The register does not tell you which arrow drove the number up.
    focus c4 a4

There is a real dispute here: critics say activity-based financing creates an incentive to code more and heavier; defenders say better coding just makes visible disease that was always there.
    highlight c2 effect glow

It cannot be settled inside the register — we lack an independent measure of disease to hold the codes against.

    clear

Back to the figure, and to what you can actually sign your name to.
    show title axes line_1 line_2 line_3 line_4

The flat line is your anchor: no rise in new users, while registrations grow by fifty-eight percent.
    highlight line_4 effect glow

What follows takes the consequence: who gives you the key to link these registers at all — and why the plan comes before the data.

You can now see what the curve really measures: not how many fell ill, but how many times someone wrote it down.
    delivery grave

chapter: "Privacy, small-cell suppression and reproducibility"

## Who gives you the key
lang: en
voice: female
level: advanced

    text title "Who gives you the key?" x 500 y 705 font_size 38 hidden true
    box n_rek0 "REK said yes" #8a5fa8 x 240 y 430 hidden true
    box n_file0 "Linked file on pid" #2f6b8f x 740 y 430 hidden true
    arrow a_naive n_rek0 -> n_file0 #b5482e hidden true
    label lbl_naive "“…the data is mine”" above #b5482e attach_to a_naive hidden true
    mark ann_strike target a_naive kind strike hidden true
    box n_npr "npr (FHI)" #2f6b8f x 195 y 590 hidden true
    box n_lmr "lmr (FHI)" #2f6b8f x 195 y 450 hidden true
    box n_kuhr "kuhr (Helsedir.)" #2f6b8f x 195 y 310 hidden true
    box n_hds "Helsedataservice" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 450 hidden true
    text lbl_hds "one door since 2023" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 405 font_size 20 hidden true
    arrow a_npr n_npr -> n_hds hidden true
    arrow a_lmr n_lmr -> n_hds hidden true
    box n_rek "REK: ethics" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 645 hidden true
    arrow a_rek n_rek -> n_hds hidden true
    box n_disp "Dispensation" #8a5fa8 x 800 y 600 hidden true
    label lbl_disp "from confidentiality" above #8a5fa8 attach_to n_disp hidden true
    arrow a_hds n_hds -> n_disp hidden true
    box n_pid "pid file" #2f6b8f x 800 y 380 hidden true
    text lbl_pid "never the ID number" #2f6b8f x 800 y 335 font_size 20 hidden true
    arrow a_disp n_disp -> n_pid hidden true

Now that we have seen what the registers really measure, the question becomes who gave you the data in the first place.
    draw title

The picture most people carry looks like this: REK, the regional ethics committee, says yes, and then the linked file arrives.
    draw n_rek0 n_file0 a_naive lbl_naive

    pause 0.4

But REK approves your research, not the release of data — none of the three registers sends a single row because an ethics committee said yes.
    draw ann_strike

Let us draw the chain that actually applies.
    clear keep ["title"]

Our three kinds of rows are held by different custodians: npr and Legemiddelregisteret at the Norwegian Institute of Public Health, FHI, and kuhr at the Directorate of Health, Helfo's parent.
    draw n_npr n_lmr n_kuhr parallel true

Since 2023 you no longer apply to npr and lmr one by one — Helsedataservice, the national health data service, is one door in, with the authority to decide on release; kuhr still has its own door.
    draw n_hds a_npr a_lmr lbl_hds

The REK approval becomes an attachment to that application: ethics is settled first, access afterwards.
    draw n_rek a_rek

And the decisive word is dispensation from the duty of confidentiality — the patients in your cohort never consented to be in your study.
    draw n_disp a_hds lbl_disp

Without that dispensation there is no lawful path from a record row to your DataFrame.
    highlight n_disp effect glow delivery grave

The linking itself happens at the data provider, not with you: the national ID number is swapped for pid before the file ever leaves them.
    draw n_pid a_disp lbl_pid

That is why pid is stable inside your project, and completely meaningless outside it.
    box n_kode "kohort.py" x 490 y 190
    arrow a_pid n_pid -> n_kode

    pause 0.5

This one door was really meant to be just the entrance hall to something much bigger.
    clear keep ["title"]

From the late 2010s Norway planned the Health Analytics Platform, Helseanalyseplattformen: the health registers available for analysis in one place.
    arrow t_line #8f887c from.x 110 from.y 340 to.x 910 to.y 340
    circle m1 "2018" #8f887c x 230 y 340
    text l1 "Platform planned" x 230 y 420 font_size 22

In 2021 came the part that was actually delivered — Helsedataservice, the application door you just saw.
    circle m2 "2021" #8a5fa8 x 520 y 340
    text l2 "Helsedataservice opens" #8a5fa8 x 520 y 420 font_size 22

In 2023 the platform itself was shut down, after years of development and some two hundred million kroner.
    circle m3 "2023" #b5482e x 810 y 340
    text l3 "platform shut down" #b5482e x 810 y 420 font_size 22

The research side argued that slow, fragmented access is the real problem: data that is never used saves no patients.
    box d1 "“Access is too slow”" #2f6b8f x 270 y 245

The privacy side argued the opposite: gathering the whole population's health data in one place, for use without consent, is a risk in itself.
    box d2 "“Too much in one place”" #b5482e x 740 y 245

The dispute is not settled — the shutdown was above all about cost and the legal hurdles of cloud storage, not a verdict on which side was right.
    point at.ref m3 gesture circle

    clear keep ["title"]

Back to the cohort: it was released for one specific purpose, E11 from npr and A10 from lmr, to describe drug use.
    box c_kohort "kohort: E11 + A10" #2f6b8f x 280 y 470

Then you get a good idea in the middle of the analysis — cancer incidence among metformin users.
    box c_new "Cancer Registry" #8a5fa8 x 740 y 470
    arrow c_arrow c_kohort -> c_new #b5482e

You cannot simply make that link: new variables mean a new release, so a new application, a new decision and a new dispensation.
    mark c_cross target c_arrow kind cross
    label c_lbl "new application" below #b5482e attach_to c_arrow

That is why the variable list is written before the application, not after you have seen what the data could have answered.
    text c_note "Variable list before the application" x 500 y 250 font_size 26

The key to the registers is not one yes but a chain — ethics, legal basis, decision, dispensation — and a pid that only means something right here.
    delivery grave

## Where next
end_page: true

    text end_prev_kicker "Previous" x 500 y 545 font_size 24 style.opacity 0.6
    link end_prev href lecture:7 title "The numbers themselves: diabetes drug use and contacts in Norway" size 320 x 500 y 395

    link end_again href lecture:8 title "Watch again" form text open here x 500 y 160
    text end_count "8 of 8" x 500 y 95 font_size 22 style.opacity 0.6
